 backend/cmm_strength_reduction.mli | 54 +++++++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 20 deletions(-)

diff --git a/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.mli b/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.mli
index e88a6b7eae..c6676a6f25 100755
--- a/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.mli
+++ b/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.mli
@@ -25,27 +25,41 @@
  * DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.                                                  *
  ******************************************************************************)
 
-(** Strength reduction of derived induction variables.
+(** Loop optimisations on [Cmm], run on each function just before instruction
+    selection.
 
     A loop reaches [Cmm] as a [Ccatch Recursive] whose handler parameters are
-    the loop-carried values. When such a handler has an integer parameter [i]
-    that every exit either steps by a constant or resets to a duplicable value,
-    and the handler body computes [b + i] for some [b] bound outside the loop,
-    this pass carries [b + i] in a new handler parameter instead of rebuilding
-    it on every iteration: the entry edges pass [b + i0] and the stepping edges
-    pass [j + stride].
-
-    OCaml code has to write this shape. The garbage collector locates a block's
-    header from any pointer it scans, and the header sits immediately before the
-    data, so an interior pointer is not a representable value and an array walk
-    cannot hoist a moving pointer the way C can. The index form is forced by the
-    language, so the arithmetic it implies is worth removing.
-
-    Deliberately out of scope: non-constant strides, sums of two induction
-    variables, products ([b * i]), reassociation, any loop whose header is not
-    syntactically a [Ccatch Recursive], and any invariant not already bound
-    outside the loop (nothing is hoisted). Only the first eligible parameter of
-    a handler is treated as an induction variable. See the implementation for
-    the exact conditions. *)
+    the loop-carried values. On each such loop, innermost first:
+
+    - Strength reduction (on by default, [-no-cmm-strength-reduction]): a sum
+      [b + i] of a loop-invariant [b] and an affine function [i] of a counter
+      that every exit steps by a constant or resets is carried in a new loop
+      parameter, so the addition leaves the loop.
+
+    - Interior pointers ([-no-cmm-interior-pointers]): in a loop that cannot
+      reach a safepoint, the invariant part of an array address is formed once
+      before the loop, as a derived pointer ([Addr]).
+
+    - Invariant strides ([-no-cmm-invariant-strides], [-no-cmm-carried-pointers]):
+      a value [start + stride * counter] with a loop-invariant [stride], possibly
+      a derived pointer, is carried across the loop and stepped by one addition.
+      Rows of unboxed vector arrays get the same treatment.
+
+    - Loop-invariant code motion ([-no-cmm-licm]): pure, total integer bindings
+      whose operands are bound outside the loop are bound before it, when that
+      does not add a live range the loop cannot afford.
+
+    - Counted loops ([-cmm-counted-loops], off by default): a GC-free loop whose
+      termination is proved (exactly, in 64-bit wrapping arithmetic; otherwise
+      behind a check on entry and a polled copy of the loop) loses its
+      back-edge poll and polls on its exits instead, and is then given the
+      pointer passes even when the unit polls. [-cmm-unchecked-loops] does the
+      same without the proof, at the cost of liveness only.
+
+    A derived pointer is never live across a safepoint: the pointer passes
+    only act where none can occur (a unit compiled without polling, or a
+    counted loop), and the emitter rejects an [Addr] live at a frame
+    descriptor ("bad GC root"). [-dcounted-loops] prints each counted-loop
+    decision. See the implementation for the exact conditions. *)
 
 val fundecl : Cmm.fundecl -> Cmm.fundecl
